Yes, absolutely. Because D1 is using OpenID Connect (OIDC) to integrate with Okta, they already receive the Okta session identifier automatically during every successful login. They do not need to make any extra API calls or build custom logic to retrieve it.

Here is exactly how it works:

The id_token and the sid Claim
At the end of a successful OIDC login flow, Okta (the Identity Provider) returns a JSON Web Token (JWT) called the id_token to D1 (the Relying Party).

Inside the payload of that id_token, Okta includes several standard claims about the user and the authentication event. One of those standard reserved claims is the sid (Session ID).

Here is a simplified example of what the decoded id_token payload looks like inside D1's memory the moment the user successfully logs in:

JSON
{
  "iss": "https://cit.okta.com",
  "aud": "d1-client-id-123",
  "sub": "cit-okta-uid-88372",
  "auth_time": 1713139200,
  "sid": "00a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890", 
  "jti": "id-token-unique-identifier"
}
sid (Session ID): This is the exact Okta session identifier. In the OIDC specification, it is explicitly designed for session tracking and backend logout coordination.

sub (Subject): This is the user's unique Okta ID, which D1 also extracts.

Negotiation Leverage
This gives you even more ammunition for your vendor discussion. When D1 completes the Okta handshake, that id_token is already sitting in D1's memory.

To include the Okta Session ID in your proposed Webhook payload, the D1 engineering team simply needs to extract the existing sid string from the JWT they have already validated. There is no complex cross-platform integration or API fetching required; it is a basic variable extraction from an object they possess locally.
